 really well can write essays the  peasants though although he cannot do  all these things still has an advantage  and the advantage is that he was born in  the United States so  he has incorporated all the customs ways  of saying ways of being facial features  gestures experiences that the Danish  professor probably didn't have because  he did acquire knowledge he did acquire  the skill of express himself elegantly  and eloquently but he was not born there  he did not have the same experiences so  you can learn to speak almost like a  native but bear in mind that you will  never be a native speaker you can  emulate the status of a native speaker  without being one the people who get  very close to speaking and being a  native speakers are normally the people  who live 30 to 40 years in a country and  they their kids were born in that  country so they've seen the entire thing  maybe from an external perspective they  didn't go to the kindergarten but  they've seen their kids go to  kindergarten and interact with other  kids and interact with other adults and  they've seen adults themselves  interacting with kids going to school  going to high school etc those get close  to speaking and being native speakers  but again that will never be native  speakers because they were not born in  that country  so if there is one thing for which  people can say or can think that you are  a native speaker is accent if you speak  a given language very very well to the  point where people cannot tell that  you're a foreigner well that means that  you have reached a native like  pronounciation and if you want to reach  native like pronunciation in a given  language you have to take into account  other things people normally tend to  think okay well what I have to do is  focus on the text focus on sounds  replicate sounds repeat etc true but I  think that one of the most important  factors that is often neglected is that  you have to take everything into account  the entire communication tool that every  single native speaker has and by  communication tools I mean my hands ko  speed gestures my face how I'm  communicating with my face my eyes my  body everything the whole thing just to  give you a few examples  an Italian might sound like a stereotype  but we actually do that  actually this ate my kid stuff ah this  isn't Rome right what are you doing we  tend to use that especially when we ask  questions with an interrogation mark  mega stuff ah or a Spanish would say but  okay they buy some ads so you see the  entire expression get a buy some nacho  or Sackman  bits to the show a Torah or the French  would go like Bob  suppose if I loosen your mouth the poly  so you got the entire set these are just  a few samples of how different cultures  channel different ways of saying things  and and the acts of does count your  pitch your intonation your articulation  your pronunciation but the truth is your  hands your face your eyes do count and  if you want to speak and sound them pass  off as an even speaker even if you're  not pay attention to these watch movies  pay close attention how people  communicate with each other adult with  adult kid with kids pay attention to  every single detail because they do  count and you can learn these  consciously and unconsciously  [Music]  it should be fairly obvious by now that  speaking like a native or passing off  like a native is not just a matter of  sounds it's a holistic process but there  is one third factor which makes a  difference and it is cultural  understanding how many times have we  found ourselves talking to native  speakers and you've seen two native  speakers talking about stuff that you  had no idea of you know no clue and this  can refer to I don't know people on TV  or like TV series or maybe TV shows or  something to happen historical things  giraffe achill things etc etc this is  part of culture this is the part of  heritage that a given generation of  native speakers has and bear in mind a  given generation of speakers because  language learning its hues its its  cultural references they do change in  space and in time  so if we're speaking for example about  English Brits maybe don't understand  certain cultural references of Americans  for Americans certain things Ritz and  maybe the same Americans of now might  not understand things that were very  common 200 years ago that's reason why  it's so difficult to nail a language  because it changes in time it changes in  space it changes its cultural references  that's why it's so difficult when we  talk about being a native speaker  actually we should talk about being a  native speaker of that place and of that  time and that's that's the cultural  references that you will never be able  to replicate because you were born in  another time or maybe in the same time  but unfortunately in another place I  just wanted to say before we wrap up the  video that this is not supposed to be  discouraging I don't want to just put  you down and say hey you will never be a  native speaker it's just a fact you will  not be a native speaker but you can pass  office one does this happen to me many  times and I can guarantee you that even  if I'm not American I'm not French and I  will never be not German for that matter  or any other nationality I've had an  amazing time every time people thought  that I was one of them  because through sound you can actually  embrace another reality embrace another  culture and and embrace an historical  moment people communities you name it so  it's always worth speak in a foreign  language  it's always worth passing off as a  native if you can if that's your aim  apart from speaking you know at an  excellent level because it opens worlds  it makes your life richer in so many  ways you cannot even start to fathom  [Music]  so the first point to take into account  is that speaking like a native does not  equal being like a native because being  like a native is just a unique  characteristic on native speakers and  that's fine the second point to take  away is that if you want to sound like a  native speaker you should not only speak  like a native speaker that is with good  articulation and pronunciation but also  acts like one with ko gestures reactions  facial features you name it it's just  like a little bit like acting and the  third thing if you want to pass off as a  native speaker is that you should  integrate and being able to understand  the same cultural and historical  references and maybe from TV and TV  series etc then native speakers do in a  given space and in a given time if you  liked the video and you haven't  subscribed yet please subscribe and  click on the link below there's a  wonderful article waiting for you about  this very same topic with more examples  you can also listen to it you name it  there's a lot of stuff there please head  over my blog and my website you will not  regret it  [Music]

